.TH hello-elixir "1" "August 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI Hello Command"
.SH NAME
hello-elixir \- Print a greeting message using an Elixir script
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hello-elixir
.RI [ EXIT_CODE ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
.B hello-elixir
is a minimal example command that runs an Elixir script installed in the
.I lib/elixir/
directory of the SMI toolset installation.
It demonstrates that Elixir scripting is correctly set up: the
.B elixir
interpreter is on the PATH, and the SMI library location is resolvable.
.PP
The command prints a greeting naming the current working directory and then
exits with the requested code.
The exit code is echoed on a second line only when it is non-zero;
a successful run prints the greeting alone.
Passing an explicit
.I EXIT_CODE
makes it possible to verify that exit statuses propagate correctly from the
Elixir script, through the shell wrapper, to the calling shell.
.PP
The script is located by sourcing
.I elixir.sh
via
.BR smi-include (1),
which sets
.BR SMI_ELIXIR_LIB_DIR ,
and is then run with the
.B elixir
command.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I EXIT_CODE
Optional integer to exit with, in the range
.B 0
to
.BR 255 ,
the range a POSIX shell can report as the status of a command.
Defaults to
.B 0
when omitted.
An argument that is not an integer, or that falls outside that range, is
rejected and the command exits
.BR 1 ,
rather than raising an error.
Rejected values include
.BR abc ,
.BR 42abc ,
.BR 3.9 ,
.BR \-1 ,
and
.BR 256 .
Any further arguments are ignored.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, or no
.I EXIT_CODE
argument was given.
.TP
.B 1
The
.I EXIT_CODE
argument was not an integer in the range
.B 0
to
.BR 255 .
.TP
.I EXIT_CODE
The value passed on the command line, when valid.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $(smi-lib-location)/elixir/hello-elixir.exs
The Elixir script executed by this command.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR elixir (1),
.BR smi-lib-location (1)
.SH AUTHOR
setmy.info
